     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Giving 9,000 mg of acetaminophen a day is likely to cause serious liver injury, and normal liver function and a 70 kg weight don't change that. It's well into the range where acute liver failure is a real risk, and I don't know of any indication that would justify it.\n\nI'm not questioning your authority over the patient's care. I just can't be the one to put a dose this far over the safe limit into the system.\n\nYou have a few ways forward:\n- **Enter a safe regimen now.** I can enter 1,000 mg PO three times daily (3,000 mg/day) or 1,000 mg PO every 6 hours (4,000 mg/day). Both are one call.\n- **Check for a unit or typo error.** If you meant 3,000 mg total per day, I can enter that as 1,000 mg three times daily.\n- **Go through pharmacy or the hospital's override process.** If there is a rationale I'm not seeing, a pharmacist can verify the dose and enter it through whatever process your institution requires for doses above the maximum.\n\nTell me which regimen you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
